# 2000-typescript-quality-standards

This file defines code quality standards for the Riff application. Cursor uses these rules to ensure consistent code quality and type safety across the TypeScript/JavaScript codebase.

## Type Safety Rules

type-safety: ALWAYS replace `any` with `unknown` or more specific types across the codebase
**/*.{ts,tsx}

function-types: ALWAYS use properly typed function signatures instead of the `Function` type
**/*.{ts,tsx}

record-types: PREFER `Record<string, unknown>` for objects with string keys over generic object types
**/*.{ts,tsx}

domain-interfaces: ALWAYS create domain-specific interfaces for complex data structures instead of using generic types
**/*.{ts,tsx}

## Unused Variables Rules

unused-vars: ALWAYS prefix unused variables with underscore (e.g., `_variableName`)
**/*.{ts,tsx}

remove-unused: PREFER removing completely unused parameters when possible
**/*.{ts,tsx}

## External Library Integration Rules

external-lib-types: ALWAYS create custom interfaces for external libraries with incomplete type definitions
**/*.{ts,tsx}

two-step-casting: ALWAYS use two-step type assertions (`as unknown as TargetType`) for external library compatibility
**/*.{ts,tsx}

## Mock Object Rules

mock-interfaces: ALWAYS create dedicated interfaces for mock objects rather than extending actual interfaces
**/*.test.{ts,tsx}

mock-naming: ALWAYS use consistent naming patterns for mock implementations (e.g., mockWebSocketProvider)
**/*.test.{ts,tsx}

## Test Utility Rules

typed-test-utils: ALWAYS prefer typed test utilities over any-based implementations
**/*.test.{ts,tsx}, **/test/**/*.{ts,tsx}

test-return-types: ALWAYS use explicit return types for test factory functions
**/*.test.{ts,tsx}, **/test/**/*.{ts,tsx}

## Best Practices

prototype-methods: ALWAYS use `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(obj, 'key')` instead of `obj.hasOwnProperty('key')`
**/*.{ts,tsx}

es6-imports: ALWAYS use ES6 imports instead of requires
**/*.{ts,tsx}

jest-assertions: PREFER using dedicated Jest matchers like `toHaveLength()` over comparing properties directly
**/*.test.{ts,tsx}

utility-interfaces: ALWAYS create utility interfaces for common patterns (Position, Dimensions, etc.)
**/*.{ts,tsx}

## Browser API Handling

browser-apis: ALWAYS create type-safe interfaces for browser-specific features
**/*.{ts,tsx}

api-comments: ALWAYS add explanatory comments for non-standard browser APIs
**/*.{ts,tsx}

## Collaborative Editing Patterns

yjs-interfaces: ALWAYS create specific interfaces for Yjs collaborative data structures
**/yjs*.{ts,tsx}, **/*yjs*.{ts,tsx}

websocket-types: ALWAYS type event handlers properly for WebSocket communications
**/*Socket*.{ts,tsx}, **/*Websocket*.{ts,tsx}

## Reference Examples

Below are examples of how to follow these rules:

### Replace `any` with more specific types

```ts
// ❌ BAD: Using `any` type
function processData(data: any): any {
  return data.items.map(item => item.value);
}

// ✅ GOOD: Using specific interfaces and types
interface DataItem {
  value: number;
  id: string;
}

interface DataContainer {
  items: DataItem[];
}

function processData(data: DataContainer): number[] {
  return data.items.map(item => item.value);
}
```

### Use `unknown` instead of `any` for truly unknown values

```ts
// ❌ BAD: Using `any` for unknown data
function handleExternalData(data: any) {
  return data.value;  // No type safety, might cause runtime errors
}

// ✅ GOOD: Using unknown with type guards
function handleExternalData(data: unknown) {
  if (typeof data === 'object' && data !== null && 'value' in data) {
    return (data as { value: unknown }).value;
  }
  throw new Error('Invalid data format');
}
```

### Replace `Function` type with proper function signatures

```ts
// ❌ BAD: Using generic Function type
function registerCallback(callback: Function) {
  // No type safety for arguments or return value
}

// ✅ GOOD: Using specific function signature
function registerCallback(callback: (data: EventData) => void) {
  // Type-safe function parameters and return value
}
```

### Prefix unused variables with underscore

```ts
// ❌ BAD: Unused variables without prefix
function processItems(items, index, collection) {
  // Only using items
  return items.value;
}

// ✅ GOOD: Prefix unused parameters with underscore
function processItems(items, _index, _collection) {
  // Clearly indicates which parameters are intentionally unused
  return items.value;
}
```

### Use two-step type assertions for external library compatibility

```ts
// ❌ BAD: Direct type assertion
const result = externalLibCall() as ExpectedType;

// ✅ GOOD: Two-step type assertion for safer conversions
const result = externalLibCall() as unknown as ExpectedType;
```

### Create dedicated interfaces for mock objects

```ts
// ❌ BAD: Extending actual interfaces for mocks when incompatible
class MockService extends ActualService {
  // May not match actual implementation
}

// ✅ GOOD: Creating dedicated mock interfaces
interface MockService {
  getData: jest.Mock<Promise<Data>>;
  processItem: jest.Mock<void, [string]>;
}

// Implement mock with proper typing
const mockService: MockService = {
  getData: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue({ /* mock data */ }),
  processItem: jest.fn()
};
```

### Avoid direct access to Object.prototype methods

```ts
// ❌ BAD: Direct prototype method access
if (obj.hasOwnProperty('key')) {
  // Implementation
}

// ✅ GOOD: Use Object.prototype methods correctly
if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(obj, 'key')) {
  // Implementation
}
```

These rules reflect the established patterns in the Riff application codebase and should be followed to maintain consistent code quality and type safety. 